{"name":"Twin Blue","hex":"#bedbed","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"twin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Either of two people (or, less commonly, animals) who shared the same uterus at the same time; one who was born at the same birth as a sibling.","Either of two similar or closely related objects, plans, institutions, people, etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To separate, divide.","To split, part; to go away, depart."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/twin"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Twin","blocks":[{"text":"Twins are two offspring produced by the same pregnancy. Twins can be either monozygotic ('identical'), meaning that they develop from one zygote, which splits and forms two embryos, or dizygotic, meaning that each twin develops from a separate egg and each egg is fertilized by its own sperm cell. Since identical twins develop from one zygote, they will share the same chromosomal sex, while fraternal twins may or may not. In very rare cases, fraternal or (semi-) identical twins can have the same mother and different fathers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Mauch_Twins.jpg/330px-Mauch_Twins.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twin"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Red-haired_Siblings.jpg/500px-Red-haired_Siblings.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Red-haired_Siblings.jpg/500px-Red-haired_Siblings.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Adult fraternal twins","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARed-haired_Siblings.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Fraternal_twin_brothers.jpg/250px-Fraternal_twin_brothers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/Fraternal_twin_brothers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Fraternal_twin_brothers.jpg/250px-Fraternal_twin_brothers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fraternal twin brothers as babies","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFraternal_twin_brothers.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Mirjam_Weichselbraun_and_Melanie_Binder%2C_ROMY_2009.jpg/500px-Mirjam_Weichselb